  "sourceAudioAnchors": [
    "Aron distinguishes religious from political violence, then cites biblical coercion, historical persecution, medical harms, secular-country outcomes, climate policy, faith as evidence-resistant, and youth rejection.",
    "Jones demands causal attribution, offers meta-analytic benefit claims, challenges unadjusted country comparisons, disputes anti-science necessity, defines pistis as committed trust, and uses medical-trust and deliberate-misuse analogies.",
    "Cross-examination centers on placebo characterization, truth versus benefit, sanctions for dissent, competing scripture readings, unseen studies, parental rejection, and whether recurring misuse counts as causal responsibility.",
    "Audio confirms Aron did not concede Jones's causal principle: Aron denied advocating violence, and Jones agreed with that denial.",
    "Audio corrects the post-communist comparison to a less strong rise in homonegativity, not a decline, and does not establish the study design.",
    "Caption and diarization overlaps, malformed names, and clipped repetitions were treated as artifacts rather than participant defects."
  ]
